(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("obs-deep",{"obs-deep/promql-in-depth-rates-aggregation-and-recording-rules":{"html":"\u003cp>PromQL looks like a simple query language, but a handful of rules decide whether your graphs tell the truth. Counters, histograms and aggregation each have a correct pattern and a common wrong one.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>How rate and increase handle counters and resets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Aggregating without losing the labels you need\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Histogram quantiles and why you cannot average them\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ratios, offsets and the staleness rules\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Recording rules for speed and consistency\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"counters-and-rate\">Counters and rate\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A counter only goes up, so its raw value is rarely useful. Use \u003ccode>rate(metric[5m])\u003c/code> to get a per-second average and \u003ccode>increase(metric[1h])\u003c/code> for a total. The range window must hold at least two scrapes, so use four times the scrape interval or more. Always apply \u003ccode>rate\u003c/code> before \u003ccode>sum\u003c/code>: summing counters first hides resets and gives wrong answers.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"aggregation-and-labels\">Aggregation and labels\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>sum by (service)(rate(http_requests_total[5m]))\u003c/code> keeps only the service label. Use \u003ccode>by\u003c/code> to keep labels and \u003ccode>without\u003c/code> to drop a few. A ratio of two aggregations needs matching labels on both sides, or the division returns nothing. Check this when a query suddenly shows no data after a label change.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"histograms-and-quantiles\">Histograms and quantiles\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Histogram buckets are counters, so use \u003ccode>histogram_quantile(0.99, sum by (le, service)(rate(latency_bucket[5m])))\u003c/code>. Keep the \u003ccode>le\u003c/code> label when aggregating. Never average pre-computed quantiles from several instances: the result has no meaning. Bucket boundaries limit accuracy, so choose them around your target latency.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"staleness-and-gaps\">Staleness and gaps\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A series that stops appearing is marked stale after five minutes, and a missing scrape leaves a gap. Alerts should use \u003ccode>for\u003c/code> durations and functions such as \u003ccode>absent()\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>up == 0\u003c/code> to catch missing data. A query that returns no result is not the same as zero.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"recording-rules\">Recording rules\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Expensive expressions evaluated by every dashboard refresh waste CPU. A recording rule computes them on a schedule and stores the result under a new name, such as \u003ccode>service:http_requests:rate5m\u003c/code>. Alerts and dashboards then read the cheap series and stay consistent.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"app&gt;prom;prom&gt;rule;rule&gt;graf\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">PromQL in depth: rates, aggregation and recording rules\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"app\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Application\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"prom\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Prometheus\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"rule\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Recording rules\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"graf\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Dashboards and alerts\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Application to Prometheus; 2, Prometheus to Recording rules; 3, Recording rules to Dashboards and alerts.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"app\">\u003cdt>Application \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Exposes counters.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Counter resets.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"prom\">\u003cdt>Prometheus \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Scrapes and stores.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Slow queries.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"rule\">\u003cdt>Recording rules \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Precompute series.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Stale results.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"graf\">\u003cdt>Dashboards and alerts \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Read cheap series.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Heavy queries.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-dashboard-that-averaged-quantiles\">Incident: A dashboard that averaged quantiles\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>The p99 latency graph looked fine while customers complained about slow requests.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"curl -s localhost:9090/api/v1/query --data-urlencode 'query=avg(service_latency_p99_seconds)'\ncurl -s localhost:9090/api/v1/query --data-urlencode 'query=histogram_quantile(0.99, sum by (le)(rate(latency_bucket[5m])))'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -s localhost:9090/api/v1/query --data-urlencode \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;query=avg(service_latency_p99_seconds)&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">{&quot;status&quot;:&quot;success&quot;,&quot;data&quot;:{&quot;result&quot;:[{&quot;value&quot;:[1731600000,&quot;0.21&quot;]}]}}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -s localhost:9090/api/v1/query --data-urlencode \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;query=histogram_quantile(0.99, sum by (le)(rate(latency_bucket[5m])))&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">{&quot;status&quot;:&quot;success&quot;,&quot;data&quot;:{&quot;result&quot;:[{&quot;value&quot;:[1731600000,&quot;1.84&quot;]}]}}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The first query averaged the p99 of each instance, which is not the p99 of all requests. The second merges the buckets and shows a real p99 of 1.84 seconds. The dashboard was changed to use bucket aggregation and an alert was set on the correct value.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using rate on a gauge or summing before rate\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Averaging quantiles across instances\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Dropping le from a histogram aggregation\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using a range window shorter than two scrapes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Putting expensive queries on every dashboard instead of recording rules\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"counters-and-rate","text":"Counters and rate"},{"id":"aggregation-and-labels","text":"Aggregation and labels"},{"id":"histograms-and-quantiles","text":"Histograms and quantiles"},{"id":"staleness-and-gaps","text":"Staleness and gaps"},{"id":"recording-rules","text":"Recording rules"},{"id":"incident-a-dashboard-that-averaged-quantiles","text":"Incident: A dashboard that averaged quantiles"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Prometheus querying basics","u":"https://prometheus.io/docs/prometheus/latest/querying/basics/"},{"t":"Recording rules","u":"https://prometheus.io/docs/prometheus/latest/configuration/recording_rules/"},{"t":"Metric and label naming","u":"https://prometheus.io/docs/practices/naming/"}],"text":"PromQL looks like a simple query language, but a handful of rules decide whether your graphs tell the truth. Counters, histograms and aggregation each have a correct pattern and a common wrong one. What you will learn How rate and increase handle counters and resets Aggregating without losing the labels you need Histogram quantiles and why you cannot average them Ratios, offsets and the staleness rules Recording rules for speed and consistency Counters and rate A counter only goes up, so its raw value is rarely useful. Use rate(metric[5m]) to get a per-second average and increase(metric[1h]) for a total. The range window must hold at least two scrapes, so use four times the scrape interval or more. Always apply rate before sum : summing counters first hides resets and gives wrong answers. Aggregation and labels sum by (service)(rate(http_requests_total[5m])) keeps only the service label. Use by to keep labels and without to drop a few. A ratio of two aggregations needs matching labels on both sides, or the division returns nothing. Check this when a query suddenly shows no data after a label change. Histograms and quantiles Histogram buckets are counters, so use histogram_quantile(0.99, sum by (le, service)(rate(latency_bucket[5m]))) . Keep the le label when aggregating. Never average pre-computed quantiles from several instances: the result has no meaning. Bucket boundaries limit accuracy, so choose them around your target latency. Staleness and gaps A series that stops appearing is marked stale after five minutes, and a missing scrape leaves a gap. Alerts should use for durations and functions such as absent() or up == 0 to catch missing data. A query that returns no result is not the same as zero. Recording rules Expensive expressions evaluated by every dashboard refresh waste CPU. A recording rule computes them on a schedule and stores the result under a new name, such as service:http_requests:rate5m . Alerts and dashboards then read the cheap series and stay consistent. PromQL in depth: rates, aggregation and recording rules Flow in order: 1, Application to Prometheus; 2, Prometheus to Recording rules; 3, Recording rules to Dashboards and alerts. Service Observability Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Application Service What it does: Exposes counters. If it fails or is weak: Counter resets. Prometheus Observability What it does: Scrapes and stores. If it fails or is weak: Slow queries. Recording rules Step What it does: Precompute series. If it fails or is weak: Stale results. Dashboards and alerts Observability What it does: Read cheap series. If it fails or is weak: Heavy queries. Incident: A dashboard that averaged quantiles Incident The p99 latency graph looked fine while customers complained about slow requests. $ curl -s localhost:9090/api/v1/query --data-urlencode &#x27;query=avg(service_latency_p99_seconds)&#x27; {\"status\":\"success\",\"data\":{\"result\":[{\"value\":[1731600000,\"0.21\"]}]}} $ curl -s localhost:9090/api/v1/query --data-urlencode &#x27;query=histogram_quantile(0.99, sum by (le)(rate(latency_bucket[5m])))&#x27; {\"status\":\"success\",\"data\":{\"result\":[{\"value\":[1731600000,\"1.84\"]}]}} (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The first query averaged the p99 of each instance, which is not the p99 of all requests. The second merges the buckets and shows a real p99 of 1.84 seconds. The dashboard was changed to use bucket aggregation and an alert was set on the correct value. Common mistakes Using rate on a gauge or summing before rate Averaging quantiles across instances Dropping le from a histogram aggregation Using a range window shorter than two scrapes Putting expensive queries on every dashboard instead of recording rules"},"obs-deep/scaling-prometheus-ha-federation-and-long-term-storage":{"html":"\u003cp>A single Prometheus is easy to start and hard to keep happy as series counts grow. Scaling means choosing how to make it highly available, how to split the load and where to keep long-term data.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What limits a single Prometheus: series, scrape load and memory\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>High availability with replicas and deduplication\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Sharding and federation, and when each fits\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Remote write and long-term storage with Thanos or Mimir\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Capacity signals to watch\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-limits-one-server\">What limits one server\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Memory use is driven mostly by active series, and each series costs a few kilobytes. Query load and the write ahead log also matter. A server with a few million active series needs tens of gigabytes. Watch \u003ccode>prometheus_tsdb_head_series\u003c/code> and scrape duration to see headroom before it runs out.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"high-availability\">High availability\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Run two identical replicas that scrape the same targets. Each has its own data, so a failure of one does not lose recent metrics. Alertmanager deduplicates alerts from both. Queries use a proxy such as Thanos Query that merges and deduplicates the replicas.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"sharding-and-federation\">Sharding and federation\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>When one server cannot hold all series, shard targets across servers by a hash or by team. Federation lets a global server scrape selected aggregated series from others, which suits a summary view. It does not replace long-term storage, and federating raw series defeats the purpose.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"long-term-storage\">Long-term storage\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Thanos sidecars upload blocks to object storage and a store gateway serves them back for queries. Mimir and similar systems accept remote write and store data centrally with horizontal scaling. Both give months of retention at lower cost, and both need downsampling and compaction settings you must understand.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"capacity-signals\">Capacity signals\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Alert on head series growth, WAL replay time, rejected samples and slow rule evaluations. A cardinality explosion from a new label is the most common cause of sudden trouble, so keep a dashboard of the top series by metric name.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"t&gt;p;p&gt;q;p&gt;o;o&gt;q\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Scaling Prometheus: HA, federation and long-term storage\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"87.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"t\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Targets\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"p\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Prometheus replicas\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"q\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Query layer\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"o\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Object storage\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Targets to Prometheus replicas; 2, Prometheus replicas to Query layer; 3, Prometheus replicas to Object storage; 4, Object storage to Query layer.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"t\">\u003cdt>Targets \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Expose metrics.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Cardinality growth.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"p\">\u003cdt>Prometheus replicas \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Scrape and store.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Out of memory.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"q\">\u003cdt>Query layer \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Merges and deduplicates.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Slow fan out.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"o\">\u003cdt>Object storage \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Long term blocks.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Missing blocks.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-label-that-added-ten-million-series\">Incident: The label that added ten million series\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A deployment added a per-request id as a label and Prometheus ran out of memory in an hour.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"curl -s localhost:9090/api/v1/status/tsdb | head -c 220\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -s localhost:9090/api/v1/status/tsdb | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -c 220\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">{&quot;status&quot;:&quot;success&quot;,&quot;data&quot;:{&quot;headStats&quot;:{&quot;numSeries&quot;:11800000,&quot;numLabelPairs&quot;:15900000},&quot;seriesCountByMetricName&quot;:[{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;http_requests_total&quot;,&quot;value&quot;:10400000}]}}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The request id label created a new series for every request. The label was dropped with a relabel rule, the server was restarted from its last good state and a limit on samples per scrape was added so one target cannot do this again.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Running one server with no replica for critical alerts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Federating raw series instead of aggregates\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring series growth until memory runs out\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keeping long retention on local disk only\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Adding labels with unbounded values\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"what-limits-one-server","text":"What limits one server"},{"id":"high-availability","text":"High availability"},{"id":"sharding-and-federation","text":"Sharding and federation"},{"id":"long-term-storage","text":"Long-term storage"},{"id":"capacity-signals","text":"Capacity signals"},{"id":"incident-the-label-that-added-ten-million-series","text":"Incident: The label that added ten million series"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Prometheus storage","u":"https://prometheus.io/docs/prometheus/latest/storage/"},{"t":"Federation","u":"https://prometheus.io/docs/prometheus/latest/federation/"},{"t":"Thanos","u":"https://thanos.io/"}],"text":"A single Prometheus is easy to start and hard to keep happy as series counts grow. Scaling means choosing how to make it highly available, how to split the load and where to keep long-term data. What you will learn What limits a single Prometheus: series, scrape load and memory High availability with replicas and deduplication Sharding and federation, and when each fits Remote write and long-term storage with Thanos or Mimir Capacity signals to watch What limits one server Memory use is driven mostly by active series, and each series costs a few kilobytes. Query load and the write ahead log also matter. A server with a few million active series needs tens of gigabytes. Watch prometheus_tsdb_head_series and scrape duration to see headroom before it runs out. High availability Run two identical replicas that scrape the same targets. Each has its own data, so a failure of one does not lose recent metrics. Alertmanager deduplicates alerts from both. Queries use a proxy such as Thanos Query that merges and deduplicates the replicas. Sharding and federation When one server cannot hold all series, shard targets across servers by a hash or by team. Federation lets a global server scrape selected aggregated series from others, which suits a summary view. It does not replace long-term storage, and federating raw series defeats the purpose. Long-term storage Thanos sidecars upload blocks to object storage and a store gateway serves them back for queries. Mimir and similar systems accept remote write and store data centrally with horizontal scaling. Both give months of retention at lower cost, and both need downsampling and compaction settings you must understand. Capacity signals Alert on head series growth, WAL replay time, rejected samples and slow rule evaluations. A cardinality explosion from a new label is the most common cause of sudden trouble, so keep a dashboard of the top series by metric name. Scaling Prometheus: HA, federation and long-term storage Flow in order: 1, Targets to Prometheus replicas; 2, Prometheus replicas to Query layer; 3, Prometheus replicas to Object storage; 4, Object storage to Query layer. Service Observability Step Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Targets Service What it does: Expose metrics. If it fails or is weak: Cardinality growth. Prometheus replicas Observability What it does: Scrape and store. If it fails or is weak: Out of memory. Query layer Step What it does: Merges and deduplicates. If it fails or is weak: Slow fan out. Object storage Data store What it does: Long term blocks. If it fails or is weak: Missing blocks. Incident: The label that added ten million series Incident A deployment added a per-request id as a label and Prometheus ran out of memory in an hour. $ curl -s localhost:9090/api/v1/status/tsdb | head -c 220 {\"status\":\"success\",\"data\":{\"headStats\":{\"numSeries\":11800000,\"numLabelPairs\":15900000},\"seriesCountByMetricName\":[{\"name\":\"http_requests_total\",\"value\":10400000}]}} (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The request id label created a new series for every request. The label was dropped with a relabel rule, the server was restarted from its last good state and a limit on samples per scrape was added so one target cannot do this again. Common mistakes Running one server with no replica for critical alerts Federating raw series instead of aggregates Ignoring series growth until memory runs out Keeping long retention on local disk only Adding labels with unbounded values"},"obs-deep/slo-alerting-burn-rates-and-alertmanager-design":{"html":"\u003cp>Alerts on raw thresholds either miss real trouble or wake people for nothing. Alerting on the speed at which an error budget is being spent fits what users feel and gives you something to decide on.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Error budgets and burn rate\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Multi-window, multi-burn-rate alerts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Page versus ticket routing\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Alertmanager grouping, inhibition and silences\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Measuring alert quality\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"error-budget-and-burn-rate\">Error budget and burn rate\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A 99.9 percent monthly SLO leaves 0.1 percent of requests as budget. Burn rate is the speed of spending it: a burn rate of one uses exactly the whole budget in the month, and a burn rate of 14.4 uses it in about two days. Alerts on high burn rates catch real trouble early.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"multiple-windows\">Multiple windows\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A fast alert uses a short window and a high burn rate, such as 14.4 over one hour and five minutes, and pages someone. A slow alert uses a lower burn rate such as 6 over six hours and also 3 over three days, and opens a ticket. Requiring both a long and a short window stops alerts that fire after an incident is over.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"routing\">Routing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Alertmanager routes by labels to receivers. Page only for symptoms users feel and that need action now. Send everything else to tickets or chat. Include a runbook link and the dashboard in every alert so the responder starts with context.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"grouping-inhibition-and-silences\">Grouping, inhibition and silences\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Group alerts by service so one outage creates one notification. Use inhibition so a cluster down alert suppresses node level alerts. Use silences with an expiry for planned work, and record who created them and why.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"alert-quality\">Alert quality\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Review each page: was it actionable, urgent and real? Track pages per week per on-call person and the share that needed no action. Delete or fix alerts that fail that review.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"sli&gt;rule;rule&gt;am;am&gt;oc\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">SLO alerting: burn rates and Alertmanager design\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"sli\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">SLI query\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"rule\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Burn rate rules\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"am\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Alertmanager\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"oc\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">On-call\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, SLI query to Burn rate rules; 2, Burn rate rules to Alertmanager; 3, Alertmanager to On-call.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"sli\">\u003cdt>SLI query \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Error ratio.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrong denominator.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"rule\">\u003cdt>Burn rate rules \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Fast and slow windows.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Single window.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"am\">\u003cdt>Alertmanager \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Groups and routes.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Bad routing.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"oc\">\u003cdt>On-call \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Acts on pages.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Alert fatigue.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-two-hundred-pages-from-one-outage\">Incident: Two hundred pages from one outage\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A database failure triggered a separate page for every dependent service.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"amtool alert query --active | wc -l\namtool config routes show | head -4\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>amtool alert query --active | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">214\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>amtool config routes show | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -4\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Routing tree:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> receiver: pager\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> group_by: [alertname instance]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Grouping by instance meant every pod produced its own notification. Grouping by service, adding an inhibition rule from the database alert to dependent alerts and moving most conditions to ticket routes reduced the next incident to three pages.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Paging on causes such as CPU instead of user impact\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using one short window that flaps\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No runbook link in the alert\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Grouping by instance so one outage floods people\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Silences with no expiry or owner\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"error-budget-and-burn-rate","text":"Error budget and burn rate"},{"id":"multiple-windows","text":"Multiple windows"},{"id":"routing","text":"Routing"},{"id":"grouping-inhibition-and-silences","text":"Grouping, inhibition and silences"},{"id":"alert-quality","text":"Alert quality"},{"id":"incident-two-hundred-pages-from-one-outage","text":"Incident: Two hundred pages from one outage"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Alerting on SLOs","u":"https://sre.google/workbook/alerting-on-slos/"},{"t":"Alertmanager","u":"https://prometheus.io/docs/alerting/latest/alertmanager/"},{"t":"Alerting rules","u":"https://prometheus.io/docs/prometheus/latest/configuration/alerting_rules/"}],"text":"Alerts on raw thresholds either miss real trouble or wake people for nothing. Alerting on the speed at which an error budget is being spent fits what users feel and gives you something to decide on. What you will learn Error budgets and burn rate Multi-window, multi-burn-rate alerts Page versus ticket routing Alertmanager grouping, inhibition and silences Measuring alert quality Error budget and burn rate A 99.9 percent monthly SLO leaves 0.1 percent of requests as budget. Burn rate is the speed of spending it: a burn rate of one uses exactly the whole budget in the month, and a burn rate of 14.4 uses it in about two days. Alerts on high burn rates catch real trouble early. Multiple windows A fast alert uses a short window and a high burn rate, such as 14.4 over one hour and five minutes, and pages someone. A slow alert uses a lower burn rate such as 6 over six hours and also 3 over three days, and opens a ticket. Requiring both a long and a short window stops alerts that fire after an incident is over. Routing Alertmanager routes by labels to receivers. Page only for symptoms users feel and that need action now. Send everything else to tickets or chat. Include a runbook link and the dashboard in every alert so the responder starts with context. Grouping, inhibition and silences Group alerts by service so one outage creates one notification. Use inhibition so a cluster down alert suppresses node level alerts. Use silences with an expiry for planned work, and record who created them and why. Alert quality Review each page: was it actionable, urgent and real? Track pages per week per on-call person and the share that needed no action. Delete or fix alerts that fail that review. SLO alerting: burn rates and Alertmanager design Flow in order: 1, SLI query to Burn rate rules; 2, Burn rate rules to Alertmanager; 3, Alertmanager to On-call. Observability Step Service Outside party Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. SLI query Observability What it does: Error ratio. If it fails or is weak: Wrong denominator. Burn rate rules Step What it does: Fast and slow windows. If it fails or is weak: Single window. Alertmanager Service What it does: Groups and routes. If it fails or is weak: Bad routing. On-call Outside party What it does: Acts on pages. If it fails or is weak: Alert fatigue. Incident: Two hundred pages from one outage Incident A database failure triggered a separate page for every dependent service. $ amtool alert query --active | wc -l 214 $ amtool config routes show | head -4 Routing tree: receiver: pager group_by: [alertname instance] (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) Grouping by instance meant every pod produced its own notification. Grouping by service, adding an inhibition rule from the database alert to dependent alerts and moving most conditions to ticket routes reduced the next incident to three pages. Common mistakes Paging on causes such as CPU instead of user impact Using one short window that flaps No runbook link in the alert Grouping by instance so one outage floods people Silences with no expiry or owner"},"obs-deep/logs-at-scale-loki-labels-and-retention":{"html":"\u003cp>Logs are the most expensive signal to keep and the one people search under stress. A cost aware design uses a few stable labels, structured content and tiered retention.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>How Loki indexes labels and stores log lines\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choosing labels: few and stable\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>LogQL basics and filtering early\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Retention, tiers and sampling\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Linking logs to traces and metrics\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"index-labels-not-content\">Index labels, not content\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Loki indexes only labels such as namespace, app and level, and stores the log text compressed in chunks. This keeps cost low but means every query narrows by labels first and then filters the text. A good label has a small set of values that does not change per request.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"labels-to-avoid\">Labels to avoid\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Do not use user ids, request ids or pod names with churn as labels. Each unique combination creates a new stream, and thousands of tiny streams make ingestion slow and queries costly. Put such values in the log line as structured fields and filter them at query time.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"logql-basics\">LogQL basics\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A query starts with a stream selector such as \u003ccode>{app=&quot;api&quot;, env=&quot;prod&quot;}\u003c/code>, then filters with \u003ccode>|= &quot;error&quot;\u003c/code> or parses with \u003ccode>| json\u003c/code>. Filter lines first and parse later, because parsing every line is expensive. Metrics from logs, such as \u003ccode>rate({app=&quot;api&quot;} |= &quot;error&quot; [5m])\u003c/code>, support alerts where no counter exists.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"retention-and-tiers\">Retention and tiers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Keep recent logs on fast storage and older logs in cheap object storage with a retention period per tenant or stream. Audit and security logs may need a longer period, while debug logs may need a day. Sample or drop noisy health check lines at the agent.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"correlation\">Correlation\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Add a trace id to each log line and configure the data source to link it to the trace view. From a spike in a metric you can then jump to logs of that service and onward to a single slow trace.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"agent&gt;dist;dist&gt;ing;ing&gt;obj\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Logs at scale: Loki, labels and retention\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"agent\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Log agent\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"dist\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Distributor\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ing\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Ingester and index\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"obj\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Object storage\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Log agent to Distributor; 2, Distributor to Ingester and index; 3, Ingester and index to Object storage.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"agent\">\u003cdt>Log agent \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Adds labels, ships lines.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Too many labels.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"dist\">\u003cdt>Distributor \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Validates and routes.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Rate limited.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ing\">\u003cdt>Ingester and index \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Builds chunks.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Stream explosion.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"obj\">\u003cdt>Object storage \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Chunks and retention.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Cost growth.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-logs-rejected-for-too-many-streams\">Incident: Logs rejected for too many streams\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A new label caused Loki to reject writes and logs went missing during an outage.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"logcli series '{app=&quot;api&quot;}' --analyze-labels | head -4\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>logcli series \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{app=&quot;api&quot;}&#x27;\u003c/span> --analyze-labels | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -4\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Label Name  Unique Values  Found In Streams\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">pod_uid     48211          48211\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">env         3              48211\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The agent added the pod uid as a label, so every restart created a new stream and the limit on active streams was hit. Removing that label and keeping app, env and namespace restored ingestion.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using request or user ids as labels\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keeping all logs at the same retention\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Parsing every line before filtering\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Logging unstructured text that is hard to query\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No link between logs and traces\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"index-labels-not-content","text":"Index labels, not content"},{"id":"labels-to-avoid","text":"Labels to avoid"},{"id":"logql-basics","text":"LogQL basics"},{"id":"retention-and-tiers","text":"Retention and tiers"},{"id":"correlation","text":"Correlation"},{"id":"incident-logs-rejected-for-too-many-streams","text":"Incident: Logs rejected for too many streams"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Loki labels","u":"https://grafana.com/docs/loki/latest/get-started/labels/"},{"t":"Loki retention","u":"https://grafana.com/docs/loki/latest/operations/storage/retention/"},{"t":"Metric and label naming","u":"https://prometheus.io/docs/practices/naming/"}],"text":"Logs are the most expensive signal to keep and the one people search under stress. A cost aware design uses a few stable labels, structured content and tiered retention. What you will learn How Loki indexes labels and stores log lines Choosing labels: few and stable LogQL basics and filtering early Retention, tiers and sampling Linking logs to traces and metrics Index labels, not content Loki indexes only labels such as namespace, app and level, and stores the log text compressed in chunks. This keeps cost low but means every query narrows by labels first and then filters the text. A good label has a small set of values that does not change per request. Labels to avoid Do not use user ids, request ids or pod names with churn as labels. Each unique combination creates a new stream, and thousands of tiny streams make ingestion slow and queries costly. Put such values in the log line as structured fields and filter them at query time. LogQL basics A query starts with a stream selector such as {app=\"api\", env=\"prod\"} , then filters with |= \"error\" or parses with | json . Filter lines first and parse later, because parsing every line is expensive. Metrics from logs, such as rate({app=\"api\"} |= \"error\" [5m]) , support alerts where no counter exists. Retention and tiers Keep recent logs on fast storage and older logs in cheap object storage with a retention period per tenant or stream. Audit and security logs may need a longer period, while debug logs may need a day. Sample or drop noisy health check lines at the agent. Correlation Add a trace id to each log line and configure the data source to link it to the trace view. From a spike in a metric you can then jump to logs of that service and onward to a single slow trace. Logs at scale: Loki, labels and retention Flow in order: 1, Log agent to Distributor; 2, Distributor to Ingester and index; 3, Ingester and index to Object storage. Observability Step Service Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Log agent Observability What it does: Adds labels, ships lines. If it fails or is weak: Too many labels. Distributor Step What it does: Validates and routes. If it fails or is weak: Rate limited. Ingester and index Service What it does: Builds chunks. If it fails or is weak: Stream explosion. Object storage Data store What it does: Chunks and retention. If it fails or is weak: Cost growth. Incident: Logs rejected for too many streams Incident A new label caused Loki to reject writes and logs went missing during an outage. $ logcli series &#x27;{app=\"api\"}&#x27; --analyze-labels | head -4 Label Name Unique Values Found In Streams pod_uid 48211 48211 env 3 48211 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The agent added the pod uid as a label, so every restart created a new stream and the limit on active streams was hit. Removing that label and keeping app, env and namespace restored ingestion. Common mistakes Using request or user ids as labels Keeping all logs at the same retention Parsing every line before filtering Logging unstructured text that is hard to query No link between logs and traces"},"obs-deep/distributed-tracing-sampling-and-correlation":{"html":"\u003cp>A trace follows one request across services. It answers where the time went. Done carelessly, it is expensive and incomplete, so sampling and propagation need deliberate choices.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Spans, traces and context propagation\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Head sampling versus tail sampling\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Attributes, events and exemplars\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading a trace to find the slow hop\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Tracing cost and storage\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"spans-and-propagation\">Spans and propagation\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Each unit of work is a span with a start, a duration and attributes. The trace id travels between services in headers, such as the W3C traceparent header. If one service does not forward it, the trace breaks in two. Check message queues and async hops, which often lose context.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"head-sampling\">Head sampling\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The decision is made at the start of the request, for example keep one percent. It is cheap and simple, but it keeps few of the rare, slow or failing requests you most want to see.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"tail-sampling\">Tail sampling\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A collector holds the whole trace for a short period and then decides: keep all errors, keep traces slower than a threshold and keep a small share of the rest. It needs memory and all spans of a trace sent to the same collector, but gives far better data per dollar.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reading-a-trace\">Reading a trace\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Look for the longest span on the critical path, repeated spans such as the same query run many times and gaps where no span exists. A gap often means queueing or an uninstrumented hop. Compare a slow trace with a normal one.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"exemplars-and-cost\">Exemplars and cost\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Exemplars attach a trace id to a metric sample so a latency histogram can link to a real slow trace. Control cost with tail sampling, attribute limits and a retention period of days, not months.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"svcA&gt;svcB;svcA&gt;col;svcB&gt;col;col&gt;back\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Distributed tracing: sampling and correlation\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"svcA\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Service A\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"svcB\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Service B\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"col\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Collector\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"back\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Trace backend\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Service A to Service B; 2, Service A to Collector; 3, Service B to Collector; 4, Collector to Trace backend.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"svcA\">\u003cdt>Service A \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Starts the trace.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Missing header.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"svcB\">\u003cdt>Service B \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Continues context.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Drops context.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"col\">\u003cdt>Collector \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Tail sampling.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Memory pressure.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"back\">\u003cdt>Trace backend \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Stores and queries.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Cost growth.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-traces-that-ended-at-the-queue\">Incident: Traces that ended at the queue\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Slow checkouts could not be traced past the message queue.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl logs deploy/otel-collector | grep -c &quot;orphan span&quot;\nkubectl logs deploy/checkout | grep traceparent | head -2\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl logs deploy/otel-collector | grep -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;orphan span&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">1840\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl logs deploy/checkout | grep traceparent | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">no traceparent header found on consumed message\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The producer did not copy the trace context into the message headers, so the consumer started a new trace. Adding context injection and extraction around the queue joined the traces and revealed the real delay in the payment service.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Sampling one percent at random and missing the slow requests\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting to propagate context through queues\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Putting unbounded values in span attributes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keeping traces for months\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never linking traces to logs and metrics\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"spans-and-propagation","text":"Spans and propagation"},{"id":"head-sampling","text":"Head sampling"},{"id":"tail-sampling","text":"Tail sampling"},{"id":"reading-a-trace","text":"Reading a trace"},{"id":"exemplars-and-cost","text":"Exemplars and cost"},{"id":"incident-traces-that-ended-at-the-queue","text":"Incident: Traces that ended at the queue"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"OpenTelemetry sampling","u":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/concepts/sampling/"},{"t":"Grafana Tempo","u":"https://grafana.com/docs/tempo/latest/"},{"t":"OpenTelemetry traces","u":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/concepts/signals/traces/"}],"text":"A trace follows one request across services. It answers where the time went. Done carelessly, it is expensive and incomplete, so sampling and propagation need deliberate choices. What you will learn Spans, traces and context propagation Head sampling versus tail sampling Attributes, events and exemplars Reading a trace to find the slow hop Tracing cost and storage Spans and propagation Each unit of work is a span with a start, a duration and attributes. The trace id travels between services in headers, such as the W3C traceparent header. If one service does not forward it, the trace breaks in two. Check message queues and async hops, which often lose context. Head sampling The decision is made at the start of the request, for example keep one percent. It is cheap and simple, but it keeps few of the rare, slow or failing requests you most want to see. Tail sampling A collector holds the whole trace for a short period and then decides: keep all errors, keep traces slower than a threshold and keep a small share of the rest. It needs memory and all spans of a trace sent to the same collector, but gives far better data per dollar. Reading a trace Look for the longest span on the critical path, repeated spans such as the same query run many times and gaps where no span exists. A gap often means queueing or an uninstrumented hop. Compare a slow trace with a normal one. Exemplars and cost Exemplars attach a trace id to a metric sample so a latency histogram can link to a real slow trace. Control cost with tail sampling, attribute limits and a retention period of days, not months. Distributed tracing: sampling and correlation Flow in order: 1, Service A to Service B; 2, Service A to Collector; 3, Service B to Collector; 4, Collector to Trace backend. Service Step Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Service A Service What it does: Starts the trace. If it fails or is weak: Missing header. Service B Service What it does: Continues context. If it fails or is weak: Drops context. Collector Step What it does: Tail sampling. If it fails or is weak: Memory pressure. Trace backend Data store What it does: Stores and queries. If it fails or is weak: Cost growth. Incident: Traces that ended at the queue Incident Slow checkouts could not be traced past the message queue. $ kubectl logs deploy/otel-collector | grep -c \"orphan span\" 1840 $ kubectl logs deploy/checkout | grep traceparent | head -2 no traceparent header found on consumed message (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The producer did not copy the trace context into the message headers, so the consumer started a new trace. Adding context injection and extraction around the queue joined the traces and revealed the real delay in the payment service. Common mistakes Sampling one percent at random and missing the slow requests Forgetting to propagate context through queues Putting unbounded values in span attributes Keeping traces for months Never linking traces to logs and metrics"},"obs-deep/observability-as-code-and-cost-control":{"html":"\u003cp>Observability grows by accident when each team adds metrics and dashboards by hand. Treating it as code gives review, repeatability and a way to control cost.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Dashboards and alerts as code\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Collector pipelines in configuration\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ownership, labels and budgets for telemetry\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading the cost by signal\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A review habit that prunes\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"dashboards-and-alerts-as-code\">Dashboards and alerts as code\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Store Grafana dashboards as JSON or generated from a library, and alert rules as files, in version control. Deploy them through a pipeline. Reviews catch broken queries and give every change an author. Hand edits in the UI are overwritten at the next deploy, so teams must know that.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"collector-pipelines\">Collector pipelines\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An OpenTelemetry Collector configuration lists receivers, processors and exporters. Use it to add resource attributes, drop noisy metrics, batch data and route to different backends. Keep the configuration in the same repository as the rules and test it with a staging collector.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"ownership-and-budgets\">Ownership and budgets\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Every metric, log stream and dashboard should have an owner label or folder. Give each team a budget in series, log gigabytes a day or trace spans a day, and show their usage next to it. Limits at the collector stop a single mistake from becoming a large bill.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reading-cost-by-signal\">Reading cost by signal\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Cost comes from active series for metrics, ingested gigabytes for logs and sampled spans for traces. Find the top ten contributors in each. Often a few metrics with unbounded labels or a chatty debug log make most of the cost.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"pruning-review\">Pruning review\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Each quarter, list dashboards with no views and alerts that never fired or always fire, and decide to delete or fix them. Track the telemetry cost per request or per customer so growth is visible against traffic.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"git&gt;ci;ci&gt;col;col&gt;cost\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Observability as code and cost control\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"git\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Repository\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ci\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pipeline\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"col\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Collector\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cost\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Cost report\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Repository to Pipeline; 2, Pipeline to Collector; 3, Collector to Cost report.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"git\">\u003cdt>Repository \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Rules and dashboards.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Hand edits.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ci\">\u003cdt>Pipeline \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Validates and deploys.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No tests.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"col\">\u003cdt>Collector \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Limits and routes.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Bad config.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cost\">\u003cdt>Cost report \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Usage by owner.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No owner.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-dashboard-edit-that-disappeared\">Incident: A dashboard edit that disappeared\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A team fixed a panel in the UI and the fix vanished the next day.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"git log --oneline -3 -- dashboards/payments.json\ncurl -s -u admin:$GRAFANA_PASS localhost:3000/api/dashboards/uid/payments | grep -c version\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>git \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">log\u003c/span> --oneline -3 -- dashboards/payments.json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">a1b2c3d Add latency panel\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -s -u admin:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$GRAFANA_PASS\u003c/span> localhost:3000/api/dashboards/uid/payments | grep -c version\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The dashboard is provisioned from the repository, so the UI edit was overwritten by the next deploy. The fix was committed as a pull request, and the provisioning settings now mark UI editing as disabled with a note linking to the repository.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Editing provisioned dashboards by hand\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No owner on metrics and dashboards\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never reviewing unused alerts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keeping debug logs at full volume in production\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Treating telemetry cost as someone else problem\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"dashboards-and-alerts-as-code","text":"Dashboards and alerts as code"},{"id":"collector-pipelines","text":"Collector pipelines"},{"id":"ownership-and-budgets","text":"Ownership and budgets"},{"id":"reading-cost-by-signal","text":"Reading cost by signal"},{"id":"pruning-review","text":"Pruning review"},{"id":"incident-a-dashboard-edit-that-disappeared","text":"Incident: A dashboard edit that disappeared"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Grafana provisioning","u":"https://grafana.com/docs/grafana/latest/administration/provisioning/"},{"t":"Metric and label naming","u":"https://prometheus.io/docs/practices/naming/"},{"t":"Prometheus storage","u":"https://prometheus.io/docs/prometheus/latest/storage/"}],"text":"Observability grows by accident when each team adds metrics and dashboards by hand. Treating it as code gives review, repeatability and a way to control cost. What you will learn Dashboards and alerts as code Collector pipelines in configuration Ownership, labels and budgets for telemetry Reading the cost by signal A review habit that prunes Dashboards and alerts as code Store Grafana dashboards as JSON or generated from a library, and alert rules as files, in version control. Deploy them through a pipeline. Reviews catch broken queries and give every change an author. Hand edits in the UI are overwritten at the next deploy, so teams must know that. Collector pipelines An OpenTelemetry Collector configuration lists receivers, processors and exporters. Use it to add resource attributes, drop noisy metrics, batch data and route to different backends. Keep the configuration in the same repository as the rules and test it with a staging collector. Ownership and budgets Every metric, log stream and dashboard should have an owner label or folder. Give each team a budget in series, log gigabytes a day or trace spans a day, and show their usage next to it. Limits at the collector stop a single mistake from becoming a large bill. Reading cost by signal Cost comes from active series for metrics, ingested gigabytes for logs and sampled spans for traces. Find the top ten contributors in each. Often a few metrics with unbounded labels or a chatty debug log make most of the cost. Pruning review Each quarter, list dashboards with no views and alerts that never fired or always fire, and decide to delete or fix them. Track the telemetry cost per request or per customer so growth is visible against traffic. Observability as code and cost control Flow in order: 1, Repository to Pipeline; 2, Pipeline to Collector; 3, Collector to Cost report. Outside party Step Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Repository Outside party What it does: Rules and dashboards. If it fails or is weak: Hand edits. Pipeline Step What it does: Validates and deploys. If it fails or is weak: No tests. Collector Observability What it does: Limits and routes. If it fails or is weak: Bad config. Cost report Observability What it does: Usage by owner. If it fails or is weak: No owner. Incident: A dashboard edit that disappeared Incident A team fixed a panel in the UI and the fix vanished the next day. $ git log --oneline -3 -- dashboards/payments.json a1b2c3d Add latency panel $ curl -s -u admin: $GRAFANA_PASS localhost:3000/api/dashboards/uid/payments | grep -c version 1 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The dashboard is provisioned from the repository, so the UI edit was overwritten by the next deploy. The fix was committed as a pull request, and the provisioning settings now mark UI editing as disabled with a note linking to the repository. Common mistakes Editing provisioned dashboards by hand No owner on metrics and dashboards Never reviewing unused alerts Keeping debug logs at full volume in production Treating telemetry cost as someone else problem"}});